  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nIn the wake of a passing overnight shortwave, weak warm air \nadvection and isentropic lift will allow for continued mostly \ncloudy conditions for today with a few isolated, light rain \nshowers, mainly for areas north and west of Albany. Despite warm\n850 hPa temperatures in place (+13 to 15C), clouds should limit\nhow warm areas get. Most areas should reach the mid-60s to \nmid-70s but some localized locations could end up warmer where \nmore breaks of sun occur. Continued warm advection tonight will \nlead to a mild night with lows mostly in the low to mid-60s.\n\nA cold front crosses the region Friday into Friday evening with\nrain showers accompanying the front. Rain showers likely begin\nduring the morning hours for areas north and west of the Capital\nRegion and slowly progress southward and eastward throughout the\nday. Latest CAMs do show the more widespread rain showers across\nnorthwestern areas with showers potentially weakening and\nbecoming more isolated/scattered in nature from the Capital\nDistrict and points south and east. Most areas look to receive\n0.25 inches of rain or less with up to 0.50 inches across the\nAdirondacks. A decent temperature gradient is expected across \nthe region on Friday due to the timing of the front with 60s for\nnorthwestern areas to the lower 80s in the mid-Hudson Valley \ninto Litchfield County, CT, where more breaks of sun are\nexpected.\n\nHigh pressure returns to start next weekend with sunny, fall-\nlike weather. Most of Sunday also looks dry as we may be split\nbetween northern and southern stream systems, but there could be\nsome showers for areas south and east of Albany with the\nsouthern stream system Sunday afternoon. Thereafter, a strong\ncold front associated with the northern stream system pushes \nthrough on Monday with the possibility for a few showers, then \nbrings a brief push of much cooler air for Tuesday and Tuesday \nnight (850 hPa temps falling below 0C). Highs Tuesday may only \nreach the 50s for most areas with lows Tuesday night in the 30s \nwith upper 20s across the Adirondacks. This chilly night may \nlead to widespread frost, including within the Hudson Valley \ninto Litchfield County where the growing season will be ongoing.",
